// run
// #1655: the `parseFloat` RESULT REGISTER. `parseFloat` lowers to `ir.RtFn`
// `parse_float`, which every backend emits as a call to `bit_rt_parse_float`
// and reads out of the FLOAT return register (`d0`/`xmm0`), because
// `seed/ir.zig` types that RtFn as returning `f64`.
//
// The Bit port of that symbol (`runtime/root/floats.bit`) declared `: u64`, so
// it returned its answer in the INTEGER register instead. Against a Bit-sourced
// `libbitrt.a` the caller then read a register the callee never wrote and EVERY
// FLOAT LITERAL IN EVERY PROGRAM FOLDED TO 0.0 — `sqrt(2) = 0`, `2^10 = 1` —
// while integer code stayed perfectly correct. `tests/rootabi.zig` gates the
// two declarations against each other; this file is the executable half, and it
// is the one that fails the moment the archive under test is the Bit one.
//
// WHY BIT PATTERNS AND NOT VALUES, and why this file cannot be written with
// `==`: `-0.0 == 0.0` is TRUE, so a value comparison passes an implementation
// that has lost the sign of zero — and losing it is exactly what a wrong
// register read looks like, since an untouched register reads as some other
// value, frequently zero. `floatBits` is the only way to observe the difference
// from Bit. `NaN != NaN` compounds it in the other direction, failing a correct
// implementation; no NaN is pinned here anyway, because the default quiet NaN's
// sign differs between x86-64 (`0xFFF8...`) and AArch64 (`0x7FF8...`) and a
// pinned constant would read as a codegen divergence.
//
// THE EXPECTED BITS ARE EXTERNAL, generated with CPython's `struct.pack('<d')`
// — IEEE binary64, and every one of these decimals is a correctly-rounded
// conversion fully determined by the standard, so the constants are portable
// across both architectures. Taking them from this repo's own formatter instead
// would make the test circular: it would pass for any implementation that is
// merely self-consistent, including a broken one.
//
// A golden case is a single file with no prelude, so `parseFloat`/`floatBits`
// are called as the predeclared primitives they are rather than through std.
function main() {
  // Ordinary finite values across the exponent range.
  print("two=${floatBits(parseFloat("2.0"))}\n")
  print("tenth=${floatBits(parseFloat("0.1"))}\n")
  print("pi=${floatBits(parseFloat("3.141592653589793"))}\n")
  print("big=${floatBits(parseFloat("1e308"))}\n")
  print("wide=${floatBits(parseFloat("1234567890.123456789"))}\n")

  // THE LOAD-BEARING ROW. `-0.0` is the one value whose correctness is
  // invisible to `==` and visible only here: a caller reading the wrong
  // register sees `+0.0` (or anything else), and `-0.0 == 0.0` would call that
  // a pass.
  print("nzero=${floatBits(parseFloat("-0.0"))}\n")
  print("zero=${floatBits(parseFloat("0.0"))}\n")
  print("neg=${floatBits(parseFloat("-2.5"))}\n")

  // The subnormal range, where the conversion is not a simple scaling: the
  // smallest positive subnormal, and one that rounds into the middle of it.
  print("sub=${floatBits(parseFloat("5e-324"))}\n")
  print("tiny=${floatBits(parseFloat("1e-300"))}\n")
  print("subnorm=${floatBits(parseFloat("1e-310"))}\n")

  // `_` separators are stripped by the conversion (SPEC §5.4), so this is the
  // same value as "1000.5".
  print("sep=${floatBits(parseFloat("1_000.5"))}\n")

  // Unparsable text yields 0.0 rather than failing — the contract
  // `runtime/root.zig` set and the Bit port matches.
  print("junk=${floatBits(parseFloat("not a number"))}\n")
}
